  'Philosophy of Science',
  'Philosophy of Science',
  'The sequence begins with philosophical tools and evidence practices, moves through the major analytic debates, then applies the framework to biology, physics, mind/cognitive science, reproducibility, and a final case study.',
  $pscijson${"slug": "philosophy-of-science", "title": "Philosophy of Science", "description": "The sequence begins with philosophical tools and evidence practices, moves through the major analytic debates, then applies the framework to biology, physics, mind/cognitive science, reproducibility, and a final case study.", "topics": [{"id": "f73c3b62-5209-4c57-8fe9-c297beeca77e", "type": "topic", "title": "Scientific Reasoning, Philosophical Method, and the Aims of Science", "description": "This opening unit asks what distinguishes scientific inquiry from other forms of disciplined reasoning. Students learn how philosophers reconstruct scientific arguments, identify assumptions, and evaluate what science is trying to achieve. Students should be able to distinguish descriptive, normative, and methodological claims about science.", "children": [{"id": "3b3e1fcb-3a3f-4625-b326-3054cee25d52", "type": "subtopic", "title": "What philosophy of science studies", "children": [{"id": "17bccc2e-551e-4c09-b3f7-15a0e6cab85a", "type": "concept", "title": "scientific method"}, {"id": "c34772ac-eca7-4fa7-a734-679cd5915e32", "type": "concept", "title": "methodology versus metaphysics"}, {"id": "1ecced50-9921-4f31-8070-ec93b6421d9c", "type": "concept", "title": "normative epistemology"}, {"id": "bfca0444-7d8c-47d9-901b-2e77ea5da1dc", "type": "concept", "title": "descriptive history of science"}, {"id": "8ebba83d-f3d6-466e-8ea9-836f19bc8df7", "type": "concept", "title": "conceptual analysis"}, {"id": "6251f0eb-a98d-4c46-afb8-97df60e3817c", "type": "concept", "title": "case-based philosophy"}], "description": "Students learn the scope of the field: not doing science directly, but examining its concepts, standards, and authority."}, {"id": "67b7eedc-65b2-4fc0-b55a-70ec73d682ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Arguments, evidence, and inference", "children": [{"id": "6f517843-9e0a-414d-aded-caa4f2750a40", "type": "concept", "title": "deduction"}, {"id": "c4088f74-7acb-4e87-b491-b34b734a0dc0", "type": "concept", "title": "induction"}, {"id": "f2abd695-84d1-482e-95be-bc4b70872f22", "type": "concept", "title": "abduction"}, {"id": "7214dac9-ce01-486f-9932-3f5f8eba06ff", "type": "concept", "title": "inference to the best explanation"}, {"id": "f64b9794-c9d8-4e0c-81b9-d4419e23e8ad", "type": "concept", "title": "argument reconstruction"}, {"id": "73368a30-8331-4f35-a837-119efa28a85e", "type": "concept", "title": "validity and soundness"}, {"id": "e14f32f9-40c4-4f10-a1de-5c31689405ca", "type": "concept", "title": "warrants and background assumptions"}], "description": "This subtopic connects the course to logic by showing how scientists move from observations to claims about unobserved structures."}, {"id": "7c2432e7-d0f8-40cd-82b3-d4fab7195a1a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Aims of science", "children": [{"id": "e3906a5d-7e14-4cb2-866d-b3a84adf3469", "type": "concept", "title": "truth"}, {"id": "ebbf9eaa-ffa8-44c9-bcb7-87241d356bda", "type": "concept", "title": "empirical adequacy"}, {"id": "a608e4dd-4241-4089-860e-968c6ce447fc", "type": "concept", "title": "prediction"}, {"id": "ecb341ab-ed28-41ca-9fe8-20faf146bf16", "type": "concept", "title": "control"}, {"id": "4a8557d2-d393-4934-827a-cc3d8b56b0e1", "type": "concept", "title": "understanding"}, {"id": "f91a3d32-482f-443a-83bd-22766c91e27f", "type": "concept", "title": "instrumental success"}, {"id": "a193f256-6007-4a56-8eec-88cf0e0e0731", "type": "concept", "title": "naturalism"}], "description": "Students compare accounts of whether science primarily seeks truth, prediction, control, understanding, explanation, or useful models."}]}, {"id": "b874c494-7d39-4a75-a40b-bcb7e875a9f8", "type": "topic", "title": "Observation, Measurement, Experiment, and Data", "description": "This unit examines how observations become evidence. It treats measurement and experiment as theory-laden, instrument-mediated practices rather than simple recordings of nature. Students should be able to analyze how data are produced, cleaned, interpreted, and made evidentially relevant.", "children": [{"id": "de5a7bb1-634e-4017-9f69-fa16c9d80c49", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Observation and theory-ladenness", "children": [{"id": "ec4d8c7a-25c9-4399-b1da-0a0c4c9e9d3c", "type": "concept", "title": "theory-ladenness"}, {"id": "e0da407b-064c-454e-8844-0ec002a2585e", "type": "concept", "title": "observation statements"}, {"id": "3a4a845f-ad78-4c66-af37-56c04d5c3fb3", "type": "concept", "title": "perceptual judgment"}, {"id": "05a0c216-d027-4425-9705-e03e398a327d", "type": "concept", "title": "instrument calibration"}, {"id": "24a8906e-55a7-473c-921c-55872b7da7a0", "type": "concept", "title": "operational definitions"}, {"id": "9a08456f-3254-40a0-abfd-d2582bfa75cd", "type": "concept", "title": "background theory"}], "description": "Students learn why observation depends on concepts, instruments, background theory, and trained perception."}, {"id": "82bf11d6-5bab-409c-a55f-e55bb93ccefc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Measurement and uncertainty", "children": [{"id": "e12e4130-5f74-4019-bf87-8b0afa3c5ba0", "type": "concept", "title": "measurement scales"}, {"id": "9e2f9b14-7138-4f2d-a147-983e84eeaf6c", "type": "concept", "title": "units"}, {"id": "aca39338-da73-4127-b5f6-52a4eb65853c", "type": "concept", "title": "precision"}, {"id": "c653cacb-74f7-43a9-bea7-e0bb30bf669c", "type": "concept", "title": "accuracy"}, {"id": "9aed8667-4347-416e-a541-3ec11c2636aa", "type": "concept", "title": "error bars"}, {"id": "c3aae34f-effd-4fde-9149-bfe1f97ed0ff", "type": "concept", "title": "systematic error"}, {"id": "50de8a48-5f01-4887-b269-579b1ad76f43", "type": "concept", "title": "random error"}, {"id": "e5830327-89af-47f3-ba67-e1d1c4e7c9ff", "type": "concept", "title": "calibration curves"}], "description": "This subtopic shows how quantities are constructed and why uncertainty is a feature of measurement, not merely a defect."}, {"id": "44bff052-ef57-467f-a1c8-7c55f775a7df", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Experiment and intervention", "children": [{"id": "f71a9483-add5-4e13-995d-b7361888fa49", "type": "concept", "title": "control groups"}, {"id": "4e81435d-c334-497f-afd3-9796b2fabd5b", "type": "concept", "title": "randomization"}, {"id": "ebbd4abd-0e2a-4d35-a391-162059ee17f5", "type": "concept", "title": "blinding"}, {"id": "5dae60ee-5053-4792-88f8-c7c598b444ec", "type": "concept", "title": "confounding"}, {"id": "d34b5bcf-732f-4a18-bd03-38912f44d21b", "type": "concept", "title": "intervention"}, {"id": "259633ad-df48-4ec2-b77f-5860626b1f1d", "type": "concept", "title": "replication"}, {"id": "e219f7f6-8e8e-4c73-87c7-b52ff43b5948", "type": "concept", "title": "negative controls"}, {"id": "8b4450c6-1a33-40cc-995e-a7d26a0ef692", "type": "concept", "title": "external validity"}], "description": "Students study the logic of experimental design and why intervention can reveal causal structure."}]}, {"id": "41a46e24-884c-48ef-9e37-a711cbaea9e0", "type": "topic", "title": "Confirmation, Induction, and Bayesian Reasoning", "description": "This topic develops the central problem of how evidence supports hypotheses. It moves from classic induction problems to contemporary probabilistic accounts of confirmation. Students should be able to explain confirmation, disconfirmation, and evidential strength in qualitative and probabilistic terms.", "children": [{"id": "7a2878da-a3ca-4dc7-a0b9-7cb3438699a0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The problem of induction", "children": [{"id": "518d8bfb-41c1-4d8b-8baa-d84b92d6620a", "type": "concept", "title": "Hume's problem"}, {"id": "6c8c8c6a-8865-4c64-9bcc-3dc9420922f1", "type": "concept", "title": "uniformity of nature"}, {"id": "7b1afd29-5475-4ab0-86a4-60a6d8af2d66", "type": "concept", "title": "enumerative induction"}, {"id": "a94f59a0-063e-4cdc-a4b0-c6b60d69584f", "type": "concept", "title": "projectibility"}, {"id": "4f75005f-ab6d-4ff9-a64c-8886b8de9442", "type": "concept", "title": "grue paradox"}, {"id": "a07eb4b0-8109-4da8-97f8-18221ae38749", "type": "concept", "title": "new riddle of induction"}], "description": "Students examine why past regularities do not logically guarantee future cases and why this matters for scientific inference."}, {"id": "be57fe1d-c134-40dd-8d99-821ca0001204", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Hypothetico-deductive confirmation", "children": [{"id": "1f255ae4-1332-4583-beb1-440b20285819", "type": "concept", "title": "prediction"}, {"id": "d27b966d-95ec-47da-8475-63fcf1829c6d", "type": "concept", "title": "auxiliary hypotheses"}, {"id": "7e64d0ad-fef0-414a-aa99-c7837d777c77", "type": "concept", "title": "Duhem-Quine problem"}, {"id": "6e435a2b-0023-426b-b36a-eee068ad6302", "type": "concept", "title": "confirming instances"}, {"id": "36a71a83-87c1-4550-8f6e-3cf1948ac0e3", "type": "concept", "title": "surprising evidence"}, {"id": "8a4bb1fb-ce21-4acc-8313-15eba1fde718", "type": "concept", "title": "ad hoc rescue"}], "description": "This subtopic studies the idea that hypotheses gain support when their observable consequences turn out true."}, {"id": "3b4d5bd7-0e91-4bf3-a265-382e2b08d62b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Bayesian confirmation", "children": [{"id": "c0664697-f694-47a4-975c-ed4e533a2996", "type": "concept", "title": "Bayes' theorem"}, {"id": "ba7a64ab-e36b-4055-abe9-dcd23382c96a", "type": "concept", "title": "prior probability"}, {"id": "a00f22fa-2b8a-430a-aee3-a41ca475e437", "type": "concept", "title": "likelihood"}, {"id": "727d6da1-f118-4fef-96f8-d2a30a9bf8bd", "type": "concept", "title": "posterior probability"}, {"id": "5423cf6b-b020-4123-9dda-3659a27396ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Bayes factor"}, {"id": "0e6966e4-df07-4de3-b990-99e054344f68", "type": "concept", "title": "base rates"}, {"id": "6a8c6a71-5ee5-4db8-b00a-f9a545289b8e", "type": "concept", "title": "confirmation measure"}, {"id": "a394d62f-deaf-4ca8-bbcf-a28c742acdbe", "type": "concept", "title": "old evidence problem"}], "description": "Students learn a formal language for updating belief in light of evidence while also seeing philosophical debates about priors."}]}, {"id": "ef399697-5aac-4418-98e1-c9d033151d06", "type": "topic", "title": "Falsification, Demarcation, and Pseudoscience", "description": "This unit asks whether science can be separated from non-science by a single criterion. Students examine falsifiability, prediction, testability, and the limits of demarcation projects. Students should be able to evaluate demarcation claims without treating them as simple slogans.", "children": [{"id": "529a0018-2a85-4c75-bff1-8d922ea12993", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Popper and falsifiability", "children": [{"id": "5af28593-5174-4f61-ae85-65e1a2749df3", "type": "concept", "title": "falsifiability"}, {"id": "4e1bd995-c496-4c07-92a4-14b664e23fa1", "type": "concept", "title": "conjectures and refutations"}, {"id": "f0e2832c-debc-43d1-87d0-f3adced4598f", "type": "concept", "title": "risky predictions"}, {"id": "7cfc75b8-f637-4109-a567-6dcc8a3b4848", "type": "concept", "title": "corroboration"}, {"id": "b79a5d21-92c2-4ccf-9743-b2a18429e05b", "type": "concept", "title": "basic statements"}, {"id": "6d67c458-1b51-4d26-b00d-39acd8f34bbe", "type": "concept", "title": "immunizing stratagems"}], "description": "Students learn Popper's challenge to verificationism and his account of bold conjectures and severe tests."}, {"id": "fa23a444-8734-4888-9979-964a03362a4b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Demarcation criteria", "children": [{"id": "9864e4dd-28c8-41f5-9cf6-eefc04c7461f", "type": "concept", "title": "testability"}, {"id": "999ff8d6-0450-4cfd-be6d-3321b8a2f8a6", "type": "concept", "title": "predictive novelty"}, {"id": "652a26d5-0775-4e31-9c38-d79a257f1ec2", "type": "concept", "title": "consilience"}, {"id": "f8830a57-5754-49ed-80f5-3d779e7b2ff5", "type": "concept", "title": "methodological naturalism"}, {"id": "d4f59e86-5bb4-41c8-855b-25abc7af820a", "type": "concept", "title": "peer criticism"}, {"id": "db77c364-b506-4b49-a3a8-4c6b3cb99bf3", "type": "concept", "title": "research programs"}, {"id": "873d817b-0be3-4b59-a5d6-6d0d7376bbc1", "type": "concept", "title": "epistemic virtues"}], "description": "This subtopic compares different markers proposed for scientific status and their weaknesses."}, {"id": "94566f8a-b183-4012-aea9-4b8c9f83e86d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Pseudoscience and public reasoning", "children": [{"id": "67928b22-ec56-44f0-8888-bd24b4795014", "type": "concept", "title": "astrology"}, {"id": "869502c3-88d5-467d-b211-6496d7013ffe", "type": "concept", "title": "creation science"}, {"id": "9ccad510-4377-4ba0-b920-3283aa7cbe58", "type": "concept", "title": "anti-vaccine claims"}, {"id": "0b0ef2c5-6b03-490f-ad41-d6dfa4c31f56", "type": "concept", "title": "conspiracy reasoning"}, {"id": "ccba49ef-4e20-41e8-9a08-aa7e4c00c844", "type": "concept", "title": "cherry picking"}, {"id": "5138e04a-f514-4b89-bdda-15e519e4dfdd", "type": "concept", "title": "moving goalposts"}, {"id": "d7fb805b-3c98-4fd8-aa17-6c0843a8f923", "type": "concept", "title": "burden of proof"}, {"id": "8ca56eb8-c248-44f4-8ff8-877078787b8c", "type": "concept", "title": "expertise"}], "description": "Students practice analyzing contested cases without reducing the issue to mere disagreement with consensus."}]}, {"id": "4c6f0d74-93a8-4f2e-b08d-eeea1567fd07", "type": "topic", "title": "Scientific Explanation and Understanding", "description": "This topic asks what it means to explain a phenomenon scientifically. Students compare covering-law, causal, mechanistic, unificationist, and pragmatic models of explanation. Students should be able to identify explanation types and assess what each reveals or hides.", "children": [{"id": "a5f03bf8-6ad1-450d-9cd3-2ed22119e297", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Covering-law explanation", "children": [{"id": "0e7cdb2d-1bae-48c7-8f49-cc425116c839", "type": "concept", "title": "deductive-nomological model"}, {"id": "41c25ccd-8788-4f38-a8cf-7ddb5137301b", "type": "concept", "title": "inductive-statistical model"}, {"id": "cc5d9dc5-df5d-4f2c-a248-366b5c6a832a", "type": "concept", "title": "explanans"}, {"id": "524e87e1-db91-4bd5-ba3e-5a00d668a8bb", "type": "concept", "title": "explanandum"}, {"id": "c2ea5b2e-d264-49fa-abda-8adb540ade8d", "type": "concept", "title": "laws"}, {"id": "22a1dd2a-69db-46d7-8ce1-4d5f00f9ca91", "type": "concept", "title": "initial conditions"}, {"id": "017d6dd4-835e-4900-937a-f74b5064f194", "type": "concept", "title": "symmetry problem"}], "description": "Students learn the classic idea that explanation subsumes events under laws and initial conditions."}, {"id": "199bf91e-4b78-452f-bd55-78f558fe03f9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Causal and mechanistic explanation", "children": [{"id": "d67fc53a-678b-44a2-aabe-021c86a6eac5", "type": "concept", "title": "causal mechanism"}, {"id": "7b409c19-b2a3-4540-907a-2c37d03de5e5", "type": "concept", "title": "component"}, {"id": "b22b3015-b242-4b16-8d7f-32a2699097dc", "type": "concept", "title": "activity"}, {"id": "4f9c2723-34c6-4f42-b568-06a0ecb8cfcc", "type": "concept", "title": "organization"}, {"id": "6608e3d6-fb67-4fa1-83a1-2b0628614859", "type": "concept", "title": "intervention"}, {"id": "6d4de3c1-d863-4a4c-8acb-e33b3c2af6a5", "type": "concept", "title": "counterfactual dependence"}, {"id": "914a0daa-0908-4895-b1bc-b7f1fbc2093e", "type": "concept", "title": "causal graph"}], "description": "This subtopic treats explanation as showing how a system produces an outcome through causal organization."}, {"id": "3988408a-d94f-42b3-85a7-bdb19dc2ee84", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Understanding and unification", "children": [{"id": "47ef8f6d-1d19-4c04-a496-ec8ac50d4a0a", "type": "concept", "title": "unification"}, {"id": "64628c45-3b8b-4fff-b287-1c6d7570ad09", "type": "concept", "title": "explanatory depth"}, {"id": "a3a5a597-a00f-42dd-9e6a-92d227b74c99", "type": "concept", "title": "why-question"}, {"id": "774b5bdc-357e-4e56-94c3-ac063dbc8954", "type": "concept", "title": "contrast class"}, {"id": "d30fb656-61a5-4886-beb8-6ced420cde31", "type": "concept", "title": "pragmatics of explanation"}, {"id": "b4961da6-13ad-4547-b058-814a200d2066", "type": "concept", "title": "scientific understanding"}], "description": "Students examine whether explanations work by reducing many phenomena to fewer patterns, models, or principles."}]}, {"id": "ddba6040-63d1-4f89-91da-8f550c2cb231", "type": "topic", "title": "Laws of Nature, Symmetry, and Modal Structure", "description": "This unit explores what laws of nature are and why they matter to explanation and prediction. It compares regularity, necessity, dispositional, and best-system accounts. Students should be able to connect laws with causation, explanation, modality, and scientific practice.", "children": [{"id": "eadad18b-7d23-488f-a438-010016bb4865", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regularity and best-system accounts", "children": [{"id": "6501011f-8ecc-444a-bddb-48e657dc7d1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Humean regularity"}, {"id": "2a1a6d26-5665-4cd6-beea-d1ae719459de", "type": "concept", "title": "best system analysis"}, {"id": "85f21f52-b0bf-4694-8c71-6dc86b6dcb3c", "type": "concept", "title": "simplicity"}, {"id": "aaa1e07c-588b-4298-a20a-e3d65d18b6ab", "type": "concept", "title": "strength"}, {"id": "a17ce0fa-5d94-496b-a359-07821aac9d05", "type": "concept", "title": "fit"}, {"id": "58bdf926-54c5-4c38-b776-dfbac29150d4", "type": "concept", "title": "mosaic"}, {"id": "80b2242a-8977-4e06-b4b6-7076fdf3eb21", "type": "concept", "title": "accidental generalization"}], "description": "Students study the view that laws summarize the best balance of simplicity and strength in describing the world."}, {"id": "6daee290-3465-4105-94fa-6cfd9ee32017", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Necessitarian and dispositional accounts", "children": [{"id": "f8830b25-bc30-4b37-9ddc-3f1d545a377c", "type": "concept", "title": "nomic necessity"}, {"id": "35024c03-2391-444a-9de7-5702eb6f9e38", "type": "concept", "title": "governing laws"}, {"id": "c21f8a95-22e3-4e2c-823c-907ac09e31c0", "type": "concept", "title": "universals"}, {"id": "da7d945f-2075-4317-9d87-bc4779d56bae", "type": "concept", "title": "powers"}, {"id": "017612b2-3381-4dcf-950e-44ec8245bfa4", "type": "concept", "title": "dispositions"}, {"id": "55ce4ad2-f10c-4923-aacc-72c96c252735", "type": "concept", "title": "counterfactual support"}, {"id": "64c5384f-fc1f-4dbc-85ae-e8e4c6a6732b", "type": "concept", "title": "modal force"}], "description": "This subtopic asks whether laws govern nature or express powers and dispositions of things."}, {"id": "099c663d-7535-4d39-bf35-4d5e45c77317", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Symmetry, conservation, and constraints", "children": [{"id": "131f759c-4507-46a7-b2f0-d5f00894fd6a", "type": "concept", "title": "symmetry"}, {"id": "1fa3b6f2-200f-4d85-b360-e77ace683132", "type": "concept", "title": "invariance"}, {"id": "ca3bc97b-ab01-4f5a-a3b3-541490148627", "type": "concept", "title": "conservation laws"}, {"id": "76a2f0ea-0733-4d1a-9c1c-595f9d015a8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Noether-style reasoning"}, {"id": "bf8e9e88-228d-4d27-8477-28bd596be0ae", "type": "concept", "title": "boundary conditions"}, {"id": "a5e1b671-8362-4586-ae87-b49de3aa437b", "type": "concept", "title": "ceteris paribus laws"}], "description": "Students see how modern science often treats laws through invariance, conservation, and structural constraints."}]}, {"id": "135cec7b-6302-4737-934b-378adbebbe3e", "type": "topic", "title": "Causation, Mechanisms, and Intervention", "description": "This topic studies causal reasoning as a bridge between metaphysics, statistics, experimentation, and scientific explanation. Students learn why correlation alone is not causation. Students should be able to evaluate causal claims using mechanisms, counterfactuals, interventions, and probabilistic evidence.", "children": [{"id": "42dcc5d1-9751-4296-9096-3442db5d6ce1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regularity, counterfactual, and probabilistic causation", "children": [{"id": "b2702a26-15f9-43eb-96da-35b8321af110", "type": "concept", "title": "constant conjunction"}, {"id": "6fe729c7-9f14-4ec2-9ced-35e838ed1a70", "type": "concept", "title": "counterfactual dependence"}, {"id": "e3e0a281-1d50-4f70-8e3f-845d01a52028", "type": "concept", "title": "probabilistic causation"}, {"id": "7f896f30-ece9-4a8a-a887-1f9327285bdf", "type": "concept", "title": "screening off"}, {"id": "9dfbcc08-8075-4486-b958-c7bfd5f43cb8", "type": "concept", "title": "common cause"}, {"id": "74f2f8d8-c393-47a3-a0c7-4566c9c21e02", "type": "concept", "title": "overdetermination"}], "description": "Students compare major accounts of what makes one event or variable a cause of another."}, {"id": "5b9ac487-eb54-43fe-a8ac-a2df7a6e2402", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interventionist causation", "children": [{"id": "ba038591-e6db-4ffd-8767-8256a3755393", "type": "concept", "title": "intervention"}, {"id": "0bc413c6-0274-40b0-a1ee-5245d4b10c2d", "type": "concept", "title": "manipulability"}, {"id": "6afc32bd-2687-4157-86dd-600752f99117", "type": "concept", "title": "structural equation model"}, {"id": "e098af41-ee89-4540-afca-7f08f7bac4c2", "type": "concept", "title": "do-operator"}, {"id": "b7bfbda8-944e-4c5d-bae2-bc311f66876c", "type": "concept", "title": "causal graph"}, {"id": "1ea73e83-8895-4726-b1ae-a96f5a525f94", "type": "concept", "title": "confounder"}, {"id": "eda9e149-5603-4da6-9652-b820f9283709", "type": "concept", "title": "mediator"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces causation as something revealed by stable changes under interventions."}, {"id": "fa3e8143-6cbe-4aa5-8dea-a0859b41807f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mechanisms across sciences", "children": [{"id": "1b3f3f22-96a8-497c-afd1-6989343c3332", "type": "concept", "title": "mechanistic explanation"}, {"id": "d44f6e67-cd88-4c19-962a-4fa6934bf3c6", "type": "concept", "title": "levels"}, {"id": "c3a65258-22ee-4a79-a596-925f6d9a9baf", "type": "concept", "title": "decomposition"}, {"id": "4766d848-9826-4574-99e3-beb65040ccef", "type": "concept", "title": "organization"}, {"id": "5695ce02-1873-422b-84be-a60d82374453", "type": "concept", "title": "robustness"}, {"id": "f9537f5e-403e-4d31-9c53-2a698233561d", "type": "concept", "title": "multi-causality"}, {"id": "dcfc2177-e351-40a0-86eb-a3e0a8bfd7e1", "type": "concept", "title": "pathway"}, {"id": "79af6dcd-6f97-43c7-8a23-08e643ed63b4", "type": "concept", "title": "feedback loop"}], "description": "Students learn why mechanisms matter differently in biology, psychology, medicine, chemistry, and social science."}]}, {"id": "514cf59f-5cd6-4795-8b3f-afc6cb3e2255", "type": "topic", "title": "Models, Idealization, Simulation, and Representation", "description": "This unit treats models as central scientific tools rather than mere simplified pictures. Students examine how models represent, distort, explain, and predict. Students should be able to analyze what a model leaves out, why that omission is justified, and how model success should be interpreted.", "children": [{"id": "1ea524c9-54ba-4c37-ad1b-b9a3363d7070", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scientific models and representation", "children": [{"id": "3feed40d-3423-428d-b2d0-5611f7c48812", "type": "concept", "title": "target system"}, {"id": "28af40ce-293a-4e51-996b-6d42f7e39de4", "type": "concept", "title": "source model"}, {"id": "cf044730-c5f7-4eaa-b7ab-052fa18b5f38", "type": "concept", "title": "similarity"}, {"id": "f74a9654-e4db-4c17-be85-1718247a12d3", "type": "concept", "title": "isomorphism"}, {"id": "bf3aff9d-a8a8-487a-8e7b-c5700397db48", "type": "concept", "title": "fictional model"}, {"id": "47a6c8eb-8d28-4e38-82a3-b775609a2d1d", "type": "concept", "title": "representational idealization"}, {"id": "bed3f5b4-bc70-4edc-a8f1-128cdda3cba4", "type": "concept", "title": "model-to-world relation"}], "description": "Students study how models stand for target systems and what makes representation accurate enough for a purpose."}, {"id": "71e3deae-6348-4f9b-94a2-bee63aa9a32b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Idealization and abstraction", "children": [{"id": "691d4ce2-e9b3-406b-b4da-eafe753309ff", "type": "concept", "title": "ideal gas"}, {"id": "f27bf1e3-9d05-4378-862f-1dbccaa0dd1e", "type": "concept", "title": "frictionless plane"}, {"id": "38dd4d02-2e3b-45f3-93a7-2d3ac4c7b110", "type": "concept", "title": "point mass"}, {"id": "25778b7e-cda8-4f04-a9d8-7b816c515ba9", "type": "concept", "title": "ceteris paribus"}, {"id": "576ca614-9122-491e-91e3-574f7c313aa8", "type": "concept", "title": "abstraction"}, {"id": "fbb0e4e1-b42f-4a87-9a32-8bcb006c6621", "type": "concept", "title": "de-idealization"}, {"id": "4c1fea93-f9cf-4b49-9fa5-1743772515e5", "type": "concept", "title": "robustness analysis"}], "description": "This subtopic shows why false assumptions can help scientific understanding when used transparently."}, {"id": "89a06f27-544d-4587-878e-d13a916577ea", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Simulation and computation", "children": [{"id": "1716a747-080b-4278-9c50-0e7c28f7849c", "type": "concept", "title": "agent-based model"}, {"id": "f80e533f-25be-4596-bd0d-ca99007692f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Monte Carlo simulation"}, {"id": "ff687b58-b6f0-4558-80c8-569fecb69573", "type": "concept", "title": "parameter sensitivity"}, {"id": "7bbe443c-4975-4271-ae92-e61b29cd713e", "type": "concept", "title": "black-box model"}, {"id": "1a79dfec-22c9-41ef-aa69-0d8e2baed8b4", "type": "concept", "title": "validation"}, {"id": "8203bcdc-7688-4f54-b980-2f57efb732c7", "type": "concept", "title": "verification"}, {"id": "2d281169-367b-4c38-a0ea-ee59731c5bac", "type": "concept", "title": "computational experiment"}], "description": "Students examine computer simulation as a mode of inquiry between theory and experiment."}]}, {"id": "3027487a-7a04-417e-b964-8fcdd2e823f8", "type": "topic", "title": "Scientific Realism and Anti-Realism", "description": "This central unit asks whether successful science gives us good reason to believe in unobservable entities and structures. Students compare realism, empiricism, instrumentalism, and structural realism. Students should be able to explain the strongest arguments on both sides of the realism debate.", "children": [{"id": "daa72ded-ed6d-4750-a7a4-900e485eea18", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Arguments for realism", "children": [{"id": "75b80a44-4e51-4f96-b7c0-6cd36a3e2378", "type": "concept", "title": "no miracles argument"}, {"id": "5e465896-c94c-4b35-b4e6-57dec5582845", "type": "concept", "title": "reference"}, {"id": "7eefd848-175f-4cf5-8a1a-9975d451aea1", "type": "concept", "title": "approximate truth"}, {"id": "cd3bd37e-4586-4504-840a-c600a48ba70e", "type": "concept", "title": "entity realism"}, {"id": "8a28a582-7489-4a3c-8dba-c327d92b1984", "type": "concept", "title": "causal manipulation"}, {"id": "7ab7249b-c7ce-44ef-b819-766c62928757", "type": "concept", "title": "novel prediction"}], "description": "Students study why predictive success seems to support belief in electrons, genes, fields, and other unobservables."}, {"id": "bcbd97a9-5ca2-4b98-be21-9d05d8116480", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Arguments against realism", "children": [{"id": "b3888445-57ad-4d9a-afbc-8bd85d89f1d9", "type": "concept", "title": "pessimistic induction"}, {"id": "b880bfb6-1987-4fce-8473-7fa6c04bc7c7", "type": "concept", "title": "underdetermination"}, {"id": "8747bada-bd95-4e82-9334-17d778b3d14c", "type": "concept", "title": "empirical adequacy"}, {"id": "91a9e4a9-1227-4203-9b08-5565a34f8759", "type": "concept", "title": "constructive empiricism"}, {"id": "64371707-579c-440b-b6b9-efcd5b29f546", "type": "concept", "title": "instrumentalism"}, {"id": "ef925367-7eb4-4ce1-abe0-396ad86dd7a5", "type": "concept", "title": "theory change"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces reasons to be cautious about belief in scientific theories beyond empirical success."}, {"id": "59ed7e7d-c4a2-4090-9c39-e80c9ca45a4f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Structural and selective realism", "children": [{"id": "8e034886-c19a-402b-b687-c9ee3dc1e87b", "type": "concept", "title": "structural realism"}, {"id": "63a4ee07-901a-4232-a632-c9658ec7c670", "type": "concept", "title": "entity realism"}, {"id": "d1876790-017b-450e-b7b2-66829baaa786", "type": "concept", "title": "selective realism"}, {"id": "595dc270-8d24-4e1d-9daf-96f022a3eed6", "type": "concept", "title": "Ramsey sentence"}, {"id": "f9e63e1d-6f47-4769-8086-78278d76a8c0", "type": "concept", "title": "observable-unobservable distinction"}, {"id": "de580b8d-b065-491a-ae94-3c419adc73d4", "type": "concept", "title": "continuity through revolution"}], "description": "Students learn compromise views that preserve what survives theory change while avoiding naive realism."}]}, {"id": "51dc8fb1-2e81-4063-924e-0fd247b44e4e", "type": "topic", "title": "Theory Change, Paradigms, and Scientific Revolutions", "description": "This topic introduces historical philosophy of science through Kuhn and later debates. Students ask how science changes when standards, problems, instruments, and concepts shift together. Students should be able to explain normal science, crisis, revolution, and incommensurability without caricature.", "children": [{"id": "5462441c-4267-4c87-9ae4-d16333e86614", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Kuhn's paradigm model", "children": [{"id": "eda3e624-6501-402f-a0cb-95d115dd3939", "type": "concept", "title": "paradigm"}, {"id": "ffa49879-276c-4048-b7e6-f3d862299d48", "type": "concept", "title": "normal science"}, {"id": "2dc386e4-0b38-466a-bb57-e6961f9f08f9", "type": "concept", "title": "puzzle solving"}, {"id": "de78605e-762b-4f46-9ecc-decf0847b5cb", "type": "concept", "title": "exemplar"}, {"id": "be14f719-0855-49cb-9e56-ed15f2f4ba92", "type": "concept", "title": "disciplinary matrix"}, {"id": "a88a403b-d053-4128-8ac5-5d01e241a9fe", "type": "concept", "title": "anomaly"}, {"id": "1556463c-15c8-4b75-a10c-74b851d6849c", "type": "concept", "title": "crisis"}], "description": "Students learn the structure of normal science and the role of exemplary problems in training scientific communities."}, {"id": "7e08e4eb-a6c3-4716-b1df-0268c574b472", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Incommensurability and rationality", "children": [{"id": "2d657ac2-d4a0-4c9b-acd6-9581ce975207", "type": "concept", "title": "meaning change"}, {"id": "1caef980-243e-4c1a-930a-4b338f53b4ee", "type": "concept", "title": "standard change"}, {"id": "85454339-8736-4745-956d-fea55688af33", "type": "concept", "title": "translation problem"}, {"id": "fb49d21c-7a0b-491a-b026-ca38d4b49ead", "type": "concept", "title": "gestalt switch"}, {"id": "ef446ab1-8c48-48ad-acc7-68611913c3f2", "type": "concept", "title": "theory choice"}, {"id": "decbbc17-7464-4e99-ab8e-7359da61622e", "type": "concept", "title": "values in theory selection"}], "description": "This subtopic examines whether theory choice across paradigms can be rational if standards shift."}, {"id": "c7b70b2a-b588-4396-af95-2ab8131d7c32", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research programs and traditions", "children": [{"id": "f9f7f96c-8850-4710-bf46-7e0b6ff221d2", "type": "concept", "title": "research program"}, {"id": "4863ffc8-3607-4288-b7e0-1c97736bc0d9", "type": "concept", "title": "hard core"}, {"id": "04c26050-2820-439d-aa85-f8f51784d6b9", "type": "concept", "title": "protective belt"}, {"id": "0e243fcf-8ef2-4ead-bff8-ce8dde1259ea", "type": "concept", "title": "progressive problemshift"}, {"id": "38c105c2-749f-4f86-a58f-59d8de39c864", "type": "concept", "title": "degenerating problemshift"}, {"id": "0c7fe1a4-203f-410e-a48c-4ae1bb3317ac", "type": "concept", "title": "research tradition"}], "description": "Students compare Kuhn with Lakatos, Laudan, and other accounts of progressive and degenerating science."}]}, {"id": "c4c58b29-b984-4859-9c89-b57c66a46fd0", "type": "topic", "title": "Objectivity, Values, and Responsible Science", "description": "This unit asks how values enter science and when they improve or distort inquiry. It treats objectivity as a social and methodological achievement rather than simple value-freedom. Students should be able to distinguish epistemic values, ethical values, social values, and policy-relevant risk judgments.", "children": [{"id": "6e375829-417e-46d4-af0c-4016afa58107", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Epistemic values in theory choice", "children": [{"id": "b05dacfa-12cf-4537-9c4f-7d7660eaf99a", "type": "concept", "title": "simplicity"}, {"id": "7b774f1e-3cd4-4e0a-ab86-884d27ea56ed", "type": "concept", "title": "scope"}, {"id": "e97c5af7-a2ee-4933-92b6-55e8ebd16e44", "type": "concept", "title": "fruitfulness"}, {"id": "03f665c5-d50c-4bb0-8fa5-9b2df2ce1e82", "type": "concept", "title": "accuracy"}, {"id": "e15d4558-a377-48a5-9eac-2d33f8c34569", "type": "concept", "title": "consistency"}, {"id": "a622b5f6-1220-4e6c-aae6-d59626c5b501", "type": "concept", "title": "coherence"}, {"id": "c7a33bad-45b0-44ec-9ccf-857a15068cf4", "type": "concept", "title": "explanatory power"}], "description": "Students learn how scientists use values that guide belief without being merely subjective preferences."}, {"id": "cbed4b3c-ac6d-419e-9952-ede596401110", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Non-epistemic values and inductive risk", "children": [{"id": "9ca8fdf3-47c2-4686-8d39-02084e9c01f9", "type": "concept", "title": "inductive risk"}, {"id": "1e92a74b-d3cf-48aa-ac87-5c05350c3bd0", "type": "concept", "title": "false positive"}, {"id": "060e7a98-fd5b-4284-b551-7e3c85dc53f8", "type": "concept", "title": "false negative"}, {"id": "9a669a7f-a793-4111-97bd-3370cb32ea80", "type": "concept", "title": "precautionary principle"}, {"id": "2b8ba9fa-09b4-4bfc-a58d-c3e80a3f9fa0", "type": "concept", "title": "risk assessment"}, {"id": "9c1a5acd-29a5-4ef7-95e3-b3c883cea876", "type": "concept", "title": "policy relevance"}, {"id": "cedda151-d036-4c08-9d2e-d2d71257f0bf", "type": "concept", "title": "value-laden science"}], "description": "This subtopic shows why evidence thresholds can depend on the consequences of being wrong."}, {"id": "e32fe6a5-7f6f-40a3-b1e5-662b23afe0bd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Objectivity as social practice", "children": [{"id": "036442d0-a850-431a-a579-9ee75c0670ee", "type": "concept", "title": "peer review"}, {"id": "78c8b649-8d0d-4217-a9ab-32e481dba3c4", "type": "concept", "title": "transformative criticism"}, {"id": "af25cf52-290e-4596-b567-c6847845f2b2", "type": "concept", "title": "standpoint"}, {"id": "6ed153de-a196-4b48-ade1-fe130a23eccc", "type": "concept", "title": "bias"}, {"id": "9bc69897-d72d-4be1-a012-f756e7501e98", "type": "concept", "title": "conflict of interest"}, {"id": "dcf43907-ed63-4db0-9e5e-daf6e76b3008", "type": "concept", "title": "open science"}, {"id": "9c8af8cd-6eeb-4974-96fb-61d2594783b0", "type": "concept", "title": "transparency"}], "description": "Students study how criticism, diversity, transparency, and replication can make inquiry more objective."}]}, {"id": "c8badd73-3f24-4cfb-8dd8-c73ef068e7dd", "type": "topic", "title": "Science, Society, Expertise, and Trust", "description": "This topic examines how scientific knowledge travels through institutions, media, courts, policy, and public life. Students analyze expertise, testimony, disagreement, and trust. Students should be able to reason about expert disagreement, public communication, and the social organization of inquiry.", "children": [{"id": "9a710ce8-06fb-4852-802b-7928330bb890", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Expertise and testimony", "children": [{"id": "78d60e1a-bccb-4870-a3a9-90517bb6f130", "type": "concept", "title": "division of cognitive labor"}, {"id": "c0912cc0-c3c9-47cb-b81d-de2e35097763", "type": "concept", "title": "credentialing"}, {"id": "201c8341-77b4-465a-9e7c-35e3eb118f23", "type": "concept", "title": "testimony"}, {"id": "8123600d-ce55-4939-b9f8-5d25c930dd94", "type": "concept", "title": "epistemic dependence"}, {"id": "cabeae03-bb86-4ad8-92b5-21140f988fae", "type": "concept", "title": "trustworthiness"}, {"id": "8059741e-f885-483d-954c-e9a0625f7b0f", "type": "concept", "title": "consensus reports"}, {"id": "804ab8e5-a77f-4bc2-a31b-423000dccd78", "type": "concept", "title": "meta-expertise"}], "description": "Students learn why most scientific knowledge is socially distributed and why testimony can be rational evidence."}, {"id": "24f699f1-53d4-40d6-be83-4300b33b9059", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scientific dissent and disagreement", "children": [{"id": "9df35dc5-2553-4363-af81-ba62efa801d8", "type": "concept", "title": "peer disagreement"}, {"id": "c062ad00-71ed-46b5-beca-4cc99d4aad4c", "type": "concept", "title": "minority view"}, {"id": "97613c01-e55e-4922-983d-fa687ebcac6f", "type": "concept", "title": "manufactured doubt"}, {"id": "4f3abc28-85da-4686-97bd-3e044748bf9b", "type": "concept", "title": "consensus"}, {"id": "c701d150-a15f-4997-8b26-6d8c9777103b", "type": "concept", "title": "replication"}, {"id": "be5a5890-6e1d-431f-94d6-9003a5182f6e", "type": "concept", "title": "conflict of interest"}, {"id": "40b4c1a9-dd05-4d3e-8d79-bcb7b7e0424a", "type": "concept", "title": "epistemic bubbles"}], "description": "This subtopic separates productive dissent from manufactured controversy or denialism."}, {"id": "e04142fe-d04c-464c-ad50-cd445db9f190", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Science communication and public reason", "children": [{"id": "f54e957e-43f5-4ee7-864c-3cb709085b2d", "type": "concept", "title": "risk communication"}, {"id": "b9ae7f25-84f4-44b4-93ca-37c8957a75d1", "type": "concept", "title": "uncertainty communication"}, {"id": "b660e1ab-441d-41a8-810e-8e9e4dc80f95", "type": "concept", "title": "deficit model"}, {"id": "1ae57e11-b30c-40cc-862b-b7516e86b586", "type": "concept", "title": "framing"}, {"id": "3d614127-669f-43df-a163-b4caa571cac2", "type": "concept", "title": "misinformation"}, {"id": "c25229a2-b701-49a9-8f06-1cb0df4a7a39", "type": "concept", "title": "science journalism"}, {"id": "e6d2d3c4-fa4f-49d9-9ca7-6d2476bf3d4e", "type": "concept", "title": "public engagement"}], "description": "Students study how evidence should be communicated under uncertainty and political pressure."}]}, {"id": "2e6de8e4-5ea2-468f-bdb5-bc9354f6de55", "type": "topic", "title": "Philosophy of Biology and Evolutionary Explanation", "description": "This special-science unit uses biology to test general philosophy of science ideas. Students examine fitness, adaptation, function, species, and biological individuality. Students should be able to apply explanation, model, causation, and realism debates to biological cases.", "children": [{"id": "75ac8aa9-f5bb-4fd5-9977-5a31108b49e7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Natural selection and adaptation", "children": [{"id": "87ef604a-1177-4c67-a52c-2cff8b4296b4", "type": "concept", "title": "fitness"}, {"id": "c5e55488-bb79-4966-9936-3c93d2f5a92c", "type": "concept", "title": "adaptation"}, {"id": "d8c376f5-f1c3-40ce-8f9c-426175cd8623", "type": "concept", "title": "selection"}, {"id": "cf42ca3d-34c8-4354-a2d5-58c0a8b2f41b", "type": "concept", "title": "drift"}, {"id": "a77b176c-42a6-4e00-9352-da735245543b", "type": "concept", "title": "trait"}, {"id": "05fa7893-ec4b-4ed3-b87f-40490810ad02", "type": "concept", "title": "environment"}, {"id": "678fab04-a64e-4ffd-8be7-d6475105b31b", "type": "concept", "title": "optimality model"}, {"id": "2fc411ef-840e-4b9d-ae4e-948f504c55c3", "type": "concept", "title": "just-so story"}], "description": "Students learn how evolutionary explanations differ from simple causal histories or teleological stories."}, {"id": "631dbcf5-784d-4819-a980-a6243afb1760", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Function and teleology", "children": [{"id": "2c2b15bc-9602-4209-90e8-da1365c8dbbe", "type": "concept", "title": "selected-effect function"}, {"id": "61b2985b-57d6-4a6a-8a9d-04c5fb81b8a2", "type": "concept", "title": "causal-role function"}, {"id": "280d8065-62fe-426e-886b-8a56344b94e8", "type": "concept", "title": "teleology"}, {"id": "885f7476-9077-4e76-a2eb-bea54dad2360", "type": "concept", "title": "homology"}, {"id": "f3c298dd-002e-4774-b974-0b1c792d33e4", "type": "concept", "title": "proper function"}, {"id": "4f5de371-18bd-4dae-8a11-428ca5541610", "type": "concept", "title": "malfunction"}, {"id": "895b354d-f3bd-450b-8ef6-3fd4dfb6607c", "type": "concept", "title": "organismal design"}], "description": "This subtopic asks what it means to say a biological part is for something."}, {"id": "83ebb291-b7c4-4ec2-8b27-a60d9d0553b4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Species, genes, and individuality", "children": [{"id": "3abc8ba4-952b-4b61-b5ec-e9d37a66b7dd", "type": "concept", "title": "species concept"}, {"id": "a0189fa1-54db-4662-be75-ff58cbddd3f0", "type": "concept", "title": "gene concept"}, {"id": "1f510cdf-770b-4254-ad0a-2d20065748e6", "type": "concept", "title": "population thinking"}, {"id": "7e52f412-f98d-4b1d-bc80-331d0bfad586", "type": "concept", "title": "biological individuality"}, {"id": "ec3d5c0f-58f5-44e6-9268-78a59e0ca31a", "type": "concept", "title": "levels of selection"}, {"id": "d4cb46a9-4a5b-438b-8959-4fd774eaebbd", "type": "concept", "title": "units of selection"}], "description": "Students examine how biological classifications challenge fixed essences and simple entity concepts."}]}, {"id": "3480e66c-432a-4857-8dfd-f56936da2b05", "type": "topic", "title": "Philosophy of Physics and the Limits of Intuition", "description": "This special-science unit explores how physics pushes philosophical questions about space, time, probability, causation, explanation, and reality beyond ordinary intuition. Students should be able to connect general philosophy of science debates with physics examples without requiring advanced physics.", "children": [{"id": "9fe9dd79-cecb-48cb-a2ac-b1ee4a1fdfef", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Space, time, and explanation", "children": [{"id": "55a2c3c0-77db-4ea4-b4de-ff6e861b6257", "type": "concept", "title": "spacetime"}, {"id": "43f9c291-ec83-4246-ab0d-1a3a9324f568", "type": "concept", "title": "simultaneity"}, {"id": "09a218b9-3f72-4e42-9c6a-31a86668c4c8", "type": "concept", "title": "relativity principle"}, {"id": "305e28cf-eaf2-409d-9410-473d8bda95ae", "type": "concept", "title": "coordinate system"}, {"id": "2ba58792-6851-462e-bf74-4ad17da04dc3", "type": "concept", "title": "invariance"}, {"id": "5b09f390-793f-4e37-b59f-df9f9b653ea2", "type": "concept", "title": "block universe"}, {"id": "f52cf60b-f443-47b4-a73d-e883f6e65bb6", "type": "concept", "title": "explanation by symmetry"}], "description": "Students study how relativity and spacetime concepts reshape questions about simultaneity, laws, and ontology."}, {"id": "78f9d96d-0956-41ea-a620-fc27aebe1c78", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Quantum theory and interpretation", "children": [{"id": "0a1d8432-e574-48a7-a34c-e4bdf4123dd3", "type": "concept", "title": "measurement problem"}, {"id": "0e000ddc-fd31-4072-a67b-947ce3f54ffa", "type": "concept", "title": "wavefunction"}, {"id": "c1dd70fb-d0bd-4bfe-8f49-9f0c22e303d1", "type": "concept", "title": "superposition"}, {"id": "a7edf230-2639-47a5-ac7b-f8a2f8e0064e", "type": "concept", "title": "Born rule"}, {"id": "ad62ff4e-04f1-4aa0-a0f5-69401428bb6b", "type": "concept", "title": "collapse"}, {"id": "4b0916cb-ca81-4183-b530-d283ff6e94b6", "type": "concept", "title": "many-worlds"}, {"id": "ef8a577e-3a72-4428-a29b-ca2369c2d204", "type": "concept", "title": "hidden variables"}, {"id": "9b263c07-855d-432b-884f-ae8da0e3c3c8", "type": "concept", "title": "Copenhagen interpretation"}], "description": "This subtopic introduces philosophical issues about measurement, probability, and ontology in quantum mechanics."}, {"id": "32eb2bfe-c648-42cb-b1df-0c9d91c7d28a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reduction, emergence, and scale", "children": [{"id": "d1d5a792-dc7d-40ed-ba5e-1b078edcc0c9", "type": "concept", "title": "reduction"}, {"id": "5d370c8c-bd88-460f-b894-e3ec8b12eed5", "type": "concept", "title": "emergence"}, {"id": "c387c968-5adc-4e64-9c2a-34225ecc5130", "type": "concept", "title": "renormalization"}, {"id": "b57b0de2-a307-4110-96cc-b15b96c7251e", "type": "concept", "title": "effective theory"}, {"id": "1cc0315c-addd-4a49-be2f-bf9347880cd3", "type": "concept", "title": "multiple realizability"}, {"id": "5e00b230-0124-4cf8-893a-b531a631554e", "type": "concept", "title": "universality"}, {"id": "1cdc7754-01cf-4da9-ba6f-36ab51fda717", "type": "concept", "title": "level of explanation"}], "description": "Students examine whether higher-level phenomena are reducible to fundamental physics or require autonomous explanations."}]}, {"id": "f8c2adff-3ac6-426e-abc2-596d57f4d350", "type": "topic", "title": "Mind, Cognitive Science, AI, and Scientific Explanation", "description": "This topic uses cognitive science and AI as case studies in explanation, modeling, representation, and mechanism. It connects philosophy of science to philosophy of mind. Students should be able to evaluate competing explanations of cognition and artificial intelligence.", "children": [{"id": "1ff42176-98c1-43ef-aef1-3e08065cf0fa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cognitive models and representation", "children": [{"id": "ce3c72bd-1664-4913-81e2-3c11cbfd8319", "type": "concept", "title": "mental representation"}, {"id": "d72a1ef6-6a4b-48c0-9390-640a25bdba7e", "type": "concept", "title": "computational model"}, {"id": "05be0550-596d-4647-8a2a-933b96c9f17f", "type": "concept", "title": "information processing"}, {"id": "1b233dc9-bd7c-467a-9b97-73376b9587ed", "type": "concept", "title": "connectionism"}, {"id": "ce60a8a1-c6e3-4dc1-8b0c-82ee4fa345de", "type": "concept", "title": "Bayesian mind"}, {"id": "8a15d70a-dc5b-4b1b-b401-555bc6676fac", "type": "concept", "title": "predictive processing"}, {"id": "44edf0d1-e7d0-4c6a-b6c9-92fafce94143", "type": "concept", "title": "behavioral data"}], "description": "Students learn how cognitive science uses models to connect behavior, computation, brain, and environment."}, {"id": "be3470d9-fb88-4b05-940c-bda2eca423d1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mechanism and levels in neuroscience", "children": [{"id": "4e3de572-a4c0-4798-84a0-764d441c2cda", "type": "concept", "title": "neural mechanism"}, {"id": "0a323949-9277-45d6-99ac-8eb6f8c95c5e", "type": "concept", "title": "localization"}, {"id": "92eecdb5-7753-4099-a8f2-c92ac3c6eebc", "type": "concept", "title": "multiple realization"}, {"id": "ce7dea82-5b7f-404a-87e6-e3f955710f0b", "type": "concept", "title": "Marr's levels"}, {"id": "b115fb9e-ddee-4a5a-a32a-a0a51f1afa03", "type": "concept", "title": "implementation"}, {"id": "ce3bb5fd-22f8-4e5a-b0cb-7ac87ae7bd8d", "type": "concept", "title": "algorithmic level"}, {"id": "b7dd43a3-7b7a-42a9-95cf-cee1631dca01", "type": "concept", "title": "computational level"}], "description": "This subtopic examines explanation across neural, psychological, and behavioral levels."}, {"id": "ffc476b8-33b6-4a28-9db3-f40016913ff9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "AI as scientific instrument and object", "children": [{"id": "c7603ee8-299e-4a15-94db-3279f77c430a", "type": "concept", "title": "machine learning"}, {"id": "57ee6968-d975-4587-a794-d2b5c16b2e09", "type": "concept", "title": "black-box model"}, {"id": "5f26b460-abee-4aa3-a6ea-616020bc1778", "type": "concept", "title": "explainable AI"}, {"id": "9e78993e-30f5-49f7-8c71-b96bf7672843", "type": "concept", "title": "benchmarking"}, {"id": "1e5ae023-f661-4ed5-bd21-d0f2f0e0968b", "type": "concept", "title": "generalization"}, {"id": "5880d1d6-44d8-4642-9b7b-c87ee557e9ac", "type": "concept", "title": "training data"}, {"id": "38b54345-512d-4a59-86d7-a35666cd56f7", "type": "concept", "title": "model interpretability"}], "description": "Students analyze AI both as a tool for science and as a domain that raises questions about explanation and understanding."}]}, {"id": "f88a7b87-804f-430d-b63a-382d9da7356e", "type": "topic", "title": "Reproducibility, Open Science, and Research Integrity", "description": "This contemporary unit treats reproducibility and integrity as philosophical, statistical, institutional, and ethical issues. Students learn how evidence can fail and how communities try to repair it. Students should be able to diagnose threats to reliability and propose better research practices.", "children": [{"id": "d808994d-f4a5-4cc3-a48d-35b7dd839e9a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Replication and reproducibility", "children": [{"id": "e42a5e94-42a7-48ab-80b1-5f93d5f92026", "type": "concept", "title": "direct replication"}, {"id": "ec65835a-e630-4f62-9e0b-22cabc3e9a81", "type": "concept", "title": "conceptual replication"}, {"id": "587ccdaf-fae7-43cf-960d-82f4332d2b50", "type": "concept", "title": "computational reproducibility"}, {"id": "aaf8c5e0-7163-4aba-9e00-1d8414c19de3", "type": "concept", "title": "robustness"}, {"id": "e958b29f-337f-4f3f-b63b-52fd1aa8877d", "type": "concept", "title": "external validity"}, {"id": "b1bd2b63-c271-4fb9-adcf-7567b328009a", "type": "concept", "title": "failed replication"}, {"id": "8eb96cad-f948-41ad-9ac4-f833eacabb71", "type": "concept", "title": "meta-analysis"}], "description": "Students distinguish repeating an experiment from reproducing an analysis and from generalizing a result."}, {"id": "07a438cd-9940-4c9a-aee3-79f84d2016e4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Questionable research practices", "children": [{"id": "58db571f-b86b-4659-b9fd-56849b1ce626", "type": "concept", "title": "p-hacking"}, {"id": "e903aa51-62f0-42d7-aa58-355744b94810", "type": "concept", "title": "HARKing"}, {"id": "392bd41d-b8a3-4be4-8a5d-5bec6484eb8f", "type": "concept", "title": "publication bias"}, {"id": "b51fcc1c-c0b0-4620-b494-bf5e83561cd5", "type": "concept", "title": "file drawer problem"}, {"id": "3f179449-dd9d-4b1e-a986-7ca2d4b47b87", "type": "concept", "title": "low power"}, {"id": "4f7b2c6f-d313-473c-9991-2a745a2b9449", "type": "concept", "title": "selective reporting"}, {"id": "f3b0556e-aab4-438b-812f-5f581324fc0a", "type": "concept", "title": "multiple comparisons"}], "description": "This subtopic examines practices that inflate evidence without necessarily involving outright fraud."}, {"id": "42969b65-7e0c-44a4-84c6-cf00bfcf9a42", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Open science reforms", "children": [{"id": "a92ccfde-033b-4831-84cf-c81aa72d62b6", "type": "concept", "title": "pre-registration"}, {"id": "4e5903b4-0908-44e2-8890-352e213735d6", "type": "concept", "title": "registered report"}, {"id": "bb77a38c-fe92-45aa-8002-bd71337a44d6", "type": "concept", "title": "open data"}, {"id": "1fb51361-33f6-4049-b942-2c78b13d045a", "type": "concept", "title": "open code"}, {"id": "95897e1a-b51a-4e5d-9e61-d9870682d6a3", "type": "concept", "title": "FAIR data"}, {"id": "92a6316d-87e5-49bd-83f1-a373c17036a7", "type": "concept", "title": "data availability statement"}, {"id": "f811b725-caec-4a4c-87e7-4d67c4ce4e4f", "type": "concept", "title": "research ethics"}], "description": "Students study how pre-registration, open data, and better incentives can improve reliability."}]}, {"id": "e9d81c44-e41a-45d1-afff-4b16cbee2434", "type": "topic", "title": "Capstone: Case Study in a Special Science", "description": "The capstone asks students to apply the course's conceptual toolkit to one mature case: climate science, medicine, epidemiology, economics, neuroscience, evolutionary biology, physics, or AI. Students should be able to integrate evidence, explanation, causation, modeling, values, and expertise into a sustained philosophical analysis.", "children": [{"id": "0af07f9c-3035-456b-996f-7a82969309c2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Selecting and framing the case", "children": [{"id": "46d8e138-8bc5-43ff-b0c7-a81f7f57a32e", "type": "concept", "title": "research question"}, {"id": "f0fd4a7c-950e-492e-b1b0-fc5f9682087b", "type": "concept", "title": "case boundary"}, {"id": "7a0a9fe6-26ee-4025-8d29-eb9f84c1fa1b", "type": "concept", "title": "background literature"}, {"id": "b0a86a99-c5a6-4058-93c2-f988379ebe7f", "type": "concept", "title": "stakeholders"}, {"id": "c8a4e2a4-8dda-4f04-8bf6-06cd8eb25a87", "type": "concept", "title": "methodological controversy"}, {"id": "08187514-ec78-4bdf-98f3-aa244afccb54", "type": "concept", "title": "conceptual issue"}], "description": "Students learn to choose a case with a clear scientific question, evidential structure, and philosophical stakes."}, {"id": "f7f9b63d-8c3d-428c-8e17-baf786dd4430", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Applying the philosophical toolkit", "children": [{"id": "b1b15d72-d7a9-420a-b864-94dd2d7dfc3a", "type": "concept", "title": "confirmation"}, {"id": "367f1088-6cb7-4854-82f4-30aab9ae2bf1", "type": "concept", "title": "causation"}, {"id": "116437eb-8543-43a3-823a-a6626deb65b8", "type": "concept", "title": "modeling"}, {"id": "fcf1d2a9-1750-42c6-8959-e29ac523ee91", "type": "concept", "title": "explanation"}, {"id": "c0eed413-7af7-4635-bfd6-c2df60dbd5a8", "type": "concept", "title": "realism"}, {"id": "6332e87a-03c0-4c96-932e-64966220f91d", "type": "concept", "title": "values"}, {"id": "4037e506-bda6-41de-9946-5d576ad08a6e", "type": "concept", "title": "expertise"}, {"id": "c5f7d0b5-f8ec-4c39-95d2-eac2548f4edb", "type": "concept", "title": "uncertainty"}], "description": "This subtopic guides students in using course concepts rather than merely summarizing the science."}, {"id": "849912e2-75d8-41b1-9900-f70224d41b4e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Writing the final interpretation", "children": [{"id": "9129c331-1f71-43bf-8cf0-424f4b6fd7cb", "type": "concept", "title": "thesis"}, {"id": "fcb07b43-f767-4670-a512-9d8c86da2c98", "type": "concept", "title": "argument map"}, {"id": "237929ac-b0d8-4972-b3b8-92ef7bb7015e", "type": "concept", "title": "objection"}, {"id": "83fd8d1e-f1e6-4841-ad9d-cb707213337d", "type": "concept", "title": "reply"}, {"id": "49eb37d1-397c-4b1b-a3d1-67cc4288a4de", "type": "concept", "title": "source integration"}, {"id": "2d1852a5-2bc5-43fe-81a1-751a25a61ce6", "type": "concept", "title": "conceptual precision"}, {"id": "08dc2c48-3f22-4917-9271-e85e0d2d7d97", "type": "concept", "title": "policy implication"}], "description": "Students learn to make an original, well-supported philosophical argument about scientific practice."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$pscijson$::jsonb,
